    "prompt": "A randomised controlled trial involving 1,747 adults tested whether high-dose vitamin D3 could affect COVID-19 outcomes. Supplementation did not change the severity of acute infection. When researchers looked at lingering symptoms eight weeks later, 21 percent of people taking vitamin D reported at least one ongoing symptom compared with 25 percent in the placebo group. The senior author called this difference a \"promising signal,\" but emphasised that larger trials are needed. The study was conducted remotely and started supplementation after infection onset, limiting interpretation.\n",
    "context": "The press piece highlights a very small absolute difference (4 percentage points) in long-COVID symptoms\nas evidence that vitamin D might help, despite no effect on acute outcomes and numerous limitations.\n",
    "expected_answer": "overclaim",
    "reference_solution": "The press piece highlights a very small absolute difference (4 percentage points) in long-COVID symptoms as evidence that vitamin D might help, despite no effect on acute outcomes and numerous limitations. This constitutes mild overclaim because the results are statistically and clinically weak.\n",
